## INTRODUCTION
Gas separation and purification are key processes in chemical production and have been widely applied in the fields of harmful gases removal,1−3 high-purity chemical production,4−6 and clean energy manufacture.7,8 The traditional gas-mixture separation techniques, such as cryogenic distillation, currently account for \(10{-}15\%\) of the world's energy consumption.9 Adsorptive separation employing suitable porous materials is a cost-effective and energy-efficient separation technology with environmental benignity and sustainability.10,11 In fact, some adsorbents such as zeolites and porous carbons have already been realized for different gas separation and purification schemes, ranging from comparatively less challenging ones such as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) to more challenging ones such as alkyne/alkene and olefin/paraffin separation.12−15
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are novel solid adsorbents that combine diverse interior functionalities as well-defined adsorptive sites and fine-tuned pore sizes for high selectivity and large capacity.16−19 The reticular chemistry in MOFs involving the building blocks approach and isoreticular principle, substituting organic linkers and metal nodes in specific, renders this type of porous materials a prominent platform for addressing various gas separation challenges.20,21 Although extensive research endeavors have been devoted to gas separations, rare examples of MOFs with high sieving effects
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are novel solid adsorbents that combine diverse interior functionalities as well-defined adsorptive sites and fine-tuned pore sizes for high selectivity and large capacity.16−19 The reticular chemistry in MOFs involving the building blocks approach and isoreticular principle, substituting organic linkers and metal nodes in specific, renders this type of porous materials a prominent platform for addressing various gas separation challenges.20,21 Although extensive research endeavors have been devoted to gas separations, rare examples of MOFs with high sieving effects have been realized, particularly for the hydrocarbon separations. For example, since the first porous MOF for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) separation reported in 2011, it took quite a lot of efforts for the community to realize a few porous MOFs for high sieving separation of relevant gas mixtures,22,23 owing to their similar molecular size (kinetic diameter: \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}3.3\,\mathrm{\AA},\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}4.2\,\mathrm{\AA}\); Figure S1). The outstanding UTSA-200 (also termed as SIFSIX-14-Cu-1) takes up significant acetylene but no ethylene particularly under low pressures of around 0.01 bar attributed to the compact pore aperture \((3.4\,\mathrm{\AA})\) and collaboratively binding sites, showing great potential for trace acetylene removal. Another ultramicroporous MOF for high sieving separation of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) is the flexible UTSA-300 \(\left(\left[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{dps})_{2}(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})\right], \mathrm{dps}=4,4^{\prime}\)-dipyridylsulfide\()\) showing better size-matching pore structures \((3.3\,\mathrm{\AA})\), which can take up acetylene under ambient pressure through gate-opening but show no observed ethylene uptake even under high saturated pressure. The negligible \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake
in UTSA-300-Zn at low pressure is accordingly unconducive for trace \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) removal for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) purification.
Although MOFs with flexible or dynamic pore structures show the potential for selective gas recognition or capture in regards to their adsorbate- or pressure-dependent gas sorption isotherms, they have rarely been explored for separation of gas mixtures.24,25 For trace impurities removal, the main issue is that typical flexible MOFs show no gas uptake at the pressures below the threshold for their structural transformations to open-pore phases (Scheme 1a), owing to the lack of robust pore space in guest-free structures. We have made some progress in addressing this challenge by virtue of flexible-robust MOFs, in which the permanent porosity of their guest-free phases enables significant gas uptakes within a substantial low-pressure range to remove trace gas impurities (Scheme 1b). For instance, the robust pore space of two-dimensional ELM-12 has been successfully made use to remove propyne from 1:99 propyne/propylene mixture26 but basically discovered from structure screening rather than result from rational design. In this regard, expanding the internal pore space of the cage-based UTSA-300-Zn that size matching with \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is of special interest. To adjust the accessibility of interior cages in UTSA-300 for trace \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), substituting metal ions with relatively large radius or elongated coordination bonds through the isoreticular approach might be a much more rational strategy for realizing novel flexible-robust MOFs for relevant gas purification.
