Herein, we report a rare example of such flexible-robust MOFs, \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{dps})_{2}(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})\right]\) (termed as NCU-100 or UTSA-300-Cu), based on isoreticular principle for trace \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) removal. The prototype UTSA-300-Zn possesses relatively small internal cages of \(3.5 \times 3.9 \times 4.1 \, \mathrm{\AA}^{3}\) that are not accessible for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules until linker rotations happen during gate-opening in \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) atmosphere at \(\sim0.2\) bar and \(298 \, \mathrm{K}\). In contrast, owing to elongated \(\mathrm{Cu-F}\) bonds, the internal cages in the \(\mathrm{Cu}\) analog are rationally increased to a size of \(3.6 \times 4.3 \times 4.2 \, \mathrm{\AA}^{3}\) that are fully accessible for \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules within a substantial low-pressure range. The resultant guest-free NCU-100a takes up significant amount of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) at 0.01 bar and \(298 \, \mathrm{K}\) (0.73 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) vs 0.04
\(\mathrm{mmol~g^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{UTSA}{-}300{-}\mathrm{Zn})\) with an increased total uptake capacity of \(4.57\,\mathrm{mmol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) and 1.0 bar, whereas it retains its inaccessibility to \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},\) demonstrating it the best porous MOF material for \(1{:}99\,\mathrm{CuH}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) separation in terms of the selectivity. Crystallography studies, structural modeling investigations, and dynamic breakthrough experiments have well illustrated the potential of this unique MOF for high sieving separation of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\).
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Slow diffusion of methanol solution of dps into the mixed aqueous solution of \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}\) and \((\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{SiF}_{6}\) at room temperature for 4 days gave the light purple rectangular prismatic single crystals of NCU-100 (Figure S2). The experimental and simulated powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns reveal the isostructural feature of NCU-100 with UTSA-300-Zn as well as its high purity (Figure S3). Single crystal X-ray diffraction revealed that NCU-100 crystallized in the orthorhombic Ibam space group (Table S1). The octahedral Cu atom is coordinated by four N atoms from four different dps linkers and two F atoms of two different \(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}\) groups, affording two-dimensional channels occupied by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules (Figure S4). The framework is further stabilized by multiple interlayer hydrogen bonds \((\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{O}{\cdots}\mathrm{F},\ 2.02{-}2.03\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) between the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) guest molecules and F atoms of \(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}\) pillars (Figure S5). By replacing the metal nodes from \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) to \({\mathrm{Cu}}\) ions, two types of slightly expanded cavities (cavity A, \(3.6\times4.3\times4.2\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) ; cavity B, \(4.7\times4.5\times4.2\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})\) with interconnected apertures of \(3.4\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) are observed in NCU-100 in contrast to the \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) analogue UTSA-300 \((3.5\times3.9\times4.1\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) and \(4.3\times4.3\times4.1\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})\) , which are better sizematching to \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule \((3.3\times3.3\times5.7\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})\) without any structural transformations. This is mainly because of the strong
Jahn−Teller effect of six-coordinated \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) rather than different metal ions radius \((0.87\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) , \(0.88\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II}))\) , as revealed by the elongated \(\mathrm{Cu-F}\) bonds \((2.27\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) in contrast to the \(Z\mathfrak{n}{-}\mathrm{F}\) bonds \((2.09\,\mathrm{\AA})\) in UTSA-300-Zn, whereas the angles between the two Cu-centered pyridyl rings \((84.2^{\circ})\) and \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) -centered angle \(\left(83.8^{\circ}\right)\) indicate similar structural distortion (Figure 1). Therefore, the solvent-accessible void volumes of undulating 2D channels across the crystallographic \(^{a b}\) -plane in NCU-100 \((30.3\%\) , Figure S4b) are slightly larger than those in UTSA- \(\cdot300{-}\mathrm{Zn}\) \((29.3\%)\) . Considering the sieving separation of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) in UTSA- \(\cdot300{-}\mathrm{Zn},\) the adjusted pore space in NCU-100 is speculated to be more conducive for trace \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) enrichment. Therefore, the activation of NCU-100 was further investigated before any potential gas separation.
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) revealed that the weight loss of guest \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules in the as-synthesized NCU-100 (below \(120\ \ ^{\circ}{\cal C})\) is about \(15.7\%\) being consistent with the theoretical value of \(15.6\%\) (Figure S6). NCU-100 could also be activated under a dynamic vacuum at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) to remove the guest \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules. Upon desolvation, the guest-free phase NCU100a (closed phase) shows shrinking interlayer spaces, while retaining its robust cage interior, as determined by Rietveld refinement (Figures 2 and S7a). The original structure of NCU100 could be restored after exposing NCU-100a in the air for 24 h. Specifically, after removal of the guest \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules, the \(\mathrm{Cu\cdotsCu}\) distance increased from 9.91 to \(10.01\,\mathring\mathrm{A},\) and the dps ligand exhibited a more twisted conformation (the Cu-centered angle between two pyridyl planes is \(87.6^{\circ}.\) ). The formation of interlayer \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking induced the ligand conformation change, and a closed phase of NCU-100a with dispersed 0D cavities (maximum cross section: \(3.8\times4.8\times5.3\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) in contrast to that of \(3.9\times4.9\times4.7\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) in UTSA-300a) was formed (Figure
The desolvated NCU-100a exhibits negligible \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake at 77 K (Figure 3a), indicating that the obtained small aperture completely blocks \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (3.64 Å) at low cryogenic temperature. However, NCU-100a displays a high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (3.3 Å) adsorption amount of \(110~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(195\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 1.0 bar (Figure 3a). It should be noted that, in low-pressure range before gate-opening, NCU-100a shows a higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity of \(23.4\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (at \(P/\) \(P_{0}=0.01\) bar, Figure S9) than that of \(8.7\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) in UTSA-300a (at \(P/P_{0}=0.07\) bar), corresponding to a larger pore volume of \(0.05\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) than that of UTSA-300a \((0.02\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\) , which
indicates a significant flexible-robust feature. The Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) specific surface area and total pore volume of NCU-100a were calculated to be \(358\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and 0.21 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. These values are higher than those of UTSA-300a (311 \(\mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.16\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ).23 The internal porosity of NCU-100a prompted us to evaluate the gas adsorption and separation performances. Impressively, NCU-100a exhibits a high \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake of \(4.57\,\mathrm{mmol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\) and 1.0 bar with a flexible-robust feature (Figure 3b),27,28 which is \(48.4\%\) higher than that of UTSA-300a \((0.98\,\mathrm{mmol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and even higher than its \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity \((3.41\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\) .23 This value sets the new-record \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity at 1 bar among MOF materials with \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) molecular sieving effect and outperforms many benchmark MOFs including UTSA-200a (3.65 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ),22 SIFSIX-2-Cu-i (4.0 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ),29 ELM-12 (2.56 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ),30 and NKMOF-1-Ni (2.72 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ),31 as summarized in Table S2. Notably, NCU-100a still completely prevents the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) entrance at the pressure up to 1.0 bar. The negligible adsorption capacity was further confirmed by the adsorption kinetic experiments using a gravimetric method. As shown in Figure S10, NCU-100a indeed does not adsorb \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) over a prolonged period of \(300\;\mathrm{min}\) .
More importantly, NCU-100a exhibits a steep \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at the low-pressure region, for example, 0.73 and \(1.05\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1.0\ \mathrm{kPa}\) and 298 and \(273\,\mathrm{~K},\) respectively, which is critically important in the application of removing trace \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (Figures 3c and S11). In contrast, the low-pressure \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake
of UTSA-300a at \(1.0\ \mathrm{kPa}\) is negligible \(\left(0.04\ \mathrm{mmol\ g^{-1}}\right)\) and results in the incompetent \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (1/99, v/v) separation. The corresponding adsorption enthalpy \(\left(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\right)\) was calculated to be \(60.5\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}\) at zero coverage (Figure S12), which is consistent with the computational predicated \(58.3\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}\). This value is slightly higher than that of UTSA-300a \((57.6\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}})\), demonstrating its stronger \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) affinity. The considerable capacity and strong binding affinity of NCU-100a toward \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) jointly make it a promising adsorbent for trace \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) removal and purification.
Impressively, the unprecedentedly high \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (1/99, v/ v) ideal adsorption solution theory (IAST) selectivity of 7291 was obtained on NCU-100a at 1.0 bar and \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) (Figure S13), which is over two orders of magnitude higher than that of UTSA-300a (27) and also higher than other state-of-the-art materials (Figure 3d). It should be pointed out that the IAST selectivity does not represent the practical \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) separation ability but for qualitative comparison. It is obvious that NCU-100a stands out as a potential porous material in the field of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) separation.
To understand the unique flexible-robust adsorption phenomenon, detailed crystallography studies were carried out. Fortunately, the single-crystallinity of NCU-100 can be well preserved after removing certain solvent from the pores, and the mild-activated sample was further exposed to the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) atmosphere at \(273\ \mathrm{K}\) for \(3\ \mathrm{h}\); we can then obtain the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\)-loaded single-crystals of \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{dps})_{2}(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})\right]\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (termed as NCU-100m\(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\)). Interestingly, in a partially desolvated structure, \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules were found orderly arranged in the pores and simultaneously bonded in the internal cage (Figure 4a). This observation clearly demonstrates that \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules could be adsorbed in the open pores of internal cages, even though the interlayer spaces are occupied by solvent molecules. Compared with NCU-100a, the dihedral pore angle between two pyridyl planes decreases from \(87.6^{\circ}\) to \(84.3^{\circ}\), and the \(\mathrm{Cu}\cdots\) Cu distance slightly decreases from 10.01 to \(9.88\ \mathrm{\AA}\). Moreover, the distance between layers expands from 3.24 to \(3.87\ \mathrm{\AA}\). In NCU-100m\(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding site in cage-like pores (Site I) demonstrates that the adsorbed \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule interacts with dual \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots F}\) hydrogen bonds between \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and two terminal F atoms of different \(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}\) units with \(\mathrm{H\cdots F}\) and \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots F}\) bound lengths of 1.71 and \(1.76\ \mathrm{\AA}\), respectively (Figure 4c).
Cu distance slightly decreases from 10.01 to \(9.88\mathrm{~\AA~}\) . Moreover, the distance between layers expands from 3.24 to \(3.87\mathrm{~\AA~}\) . In NCU- \(\mathrm{100m}\mathrm{D}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2},\) the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding site in cage-like pores (Site I) demonstrates that the adsorbed \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule interacts with dual \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots F}\) hydrogen bonds between \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and two terminal F atoms of different \(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}\) units with \(\mathrm{H\cdots F}\) and \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots}\) F bound lengths of 1.71 and \(1.76\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , respectively (Figure 4c).
Next, a fully \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\)-loaded structure of NCU-100a\(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was obtained by exposing the completely desolvated NCU-100a to the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) atmosphere for \(3\ \mathrm{h}\) at \(273\ \mathrm{K}\) and studied by powder X-ray diffraction through Rietveld refinements. After \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption, the closed-form of NCU-100a expands to its open state of NCU-100a\(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) (Figures 4b and S7b). The dihedral angle between two pyridyl planes increases from \(87.6^{\circ}\) to \(88.0^{\circ}\), and the \(\mathrm{Cu\cdotsCu}\) distance further decreases from 10.01 to \(9.61\ \mathrm{\AA}\). More importantly, the interlayer spaces could be selectively refilled by \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecules and expanded from 3.24 to \(4.53\ \mathrm{\AA}\) (Figure 4b). This phenomenon is consistent with that of UTSA-300a. Each adsorbed \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule is firmly interacted by two \(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}\) sites from different nets via cooperative \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots F}\) hydrogen-bond interaction (Figure S18). Besides Site I, in the secondary binding site (Site II), the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) molecule locates in the interlayer spaces and is asymmetrically bonded with the \(\mathrm{H\cdots F}\) \((1.86\ \mathrm{\AA})\) and \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots F}\) \((4.21\ \mathrm{\AA})\) interactions (Figure 4d). The shorter hydrogen bond distance in NCU-100a\(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) \((\mathrm{H\cdots F}, 1.71\ \mathrm{\AA})\) than that of UTSA-300a\(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}(\mathrm{D\cdots F}, 1.98\ \mathrm{\AA})\) demonstrates its stronger \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) affinity and thus higher adsorption enthalpy. In situ PXRD of NUC-100a upon different \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) partial pressures has also been performed (Figure S19), showing no intermediate state but intensity increases of diffraction peaks as the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) dose increases. We then carried out the molecular dynamics simulation to verify the structural conformations. As shown in Figure S20, the optimized \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorption locations and orientations are in agreement with the experimental results in Figure 4d. On Site I, dual \(\mathrm{C-H\cdots F}\)
hydrogen bonds notably occur between \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}\) anions \(\left(\mathrm{H}{\cdots}\mathrm{F}\ 1.72\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},\ \mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}{\cdots}\mathrm{F}\ 1.73\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)\) . On Site II, two \(\mathrm{C-H\cdotsF}\) hydrogen interactions \(\left(\mathrm{H}{\cdots}\mathrm{F}\,1.84\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}{\cdots}\mathrm{F}\,4.20\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\right)\) were also observed.
Ethylene \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}\right)\) , as an important chemical feedstock, is mainly derived from the steam cracking operation in petroleum refining processes.32 Typically, trace amounts \((\sim\!1\%)\) of acetylene \(\left(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)\) are generated and will induce the severe catalyst poison and even an explosion.33,34 Hence, removing trace amount of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) mixtures \((1/99,\mathrm{v/v})\) is urgently demanded. To further examine the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) separation performance of NCU-100a in practical applications, dynamic breakthrough experiments were conducted under ambient conditions, and gas mixtures of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (1/99 and \(50/50,\mathrm{v/v})\) were applied to a packed column of NCU-100a at a flow rate of \(2.0\,\mathrm{mL\,min^{-1}}\) and \(298\,\mathrm{K}.\) As shown in Figure 5a, \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) eluted first through the fixed bed, and then high-purity \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) product \((\geq\!99.9995\%)\) can be directly collected, whereas \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) was retained in the interior of packed adsorbent for 150 min. For comparison, the \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) productivity and purity were calculated from the breakthrough curves and compared with those of benchmark porous materials. The \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) productivity with \(99.99\%+\) purity obtained by NCU-100a is \(14.9\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , higher than that of UTSA-300a \(\left(0.49\,\mathrm{mmol}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)\) ,23 which is also superior to those of many outperforming MOFs including UTSA-100a \(\left(1.41\,\mathrm{\mmol\,\,g^{-1}}\right)\) ,35 TJT-100 \(\left(1.76\,\mathrm{\mmol\,\,g^{-1}}\right)\) ,4 UTSA-220 \(\left(3.09\,\mathrm{\mmol\,\,g^{-1}}\right)\) ,36 NbU-1 \(\left(3.97\,\mathrm{\mmol\,\,g^{-1}}\right)\) ,37
SIFSIX-3-Zn \(\left(3.68\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)\) ,29 HOF-21a \(\left(4.06\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)\) ,38 and NOTT-300 \(\left(8.98\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)\) ,39 only lower than those of UTSA-200a \(\left(85.7\;\mathrm{mmol}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)\) 22 and SIFSIX-2-Cu-i \(\left(47.4\;\mathrm{mmol}\right.\) \(\mathbf{g}^{-1})^{29}\) (Figure 5d). Moreover, the sorbent selection parameter (S) of NCU-100a has also been evaluated (Table S4 and Figure S21), revealing its great application potential in a vacuum swing adsorption (VSA) process.40
To evaluate the potential of practical application, the multiple cycling and regeneration breakthrough have been performed. As shown in Figure 5a, well-retained \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity and breakthrough time during five successive cycles jointly confirm the production stability and recyclability of NCU-100a. The adsorbent could be fully regenerated with a \(\mathrm{H}-\) flow of \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{min^{-1}}\) at \(298~\mathrm{K}\) (Figure S22). The efficient separation of \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) \((50/50,\,\mathrm{v/v})\) mixture was also observed in Figure S23. Meanwhile, the tolerance of water vapor and organic solvents is an important factor in evaluating the application potential of adsorbents. Remarkably, the clear separation was also maintained in the humid condition (Figure 5b). After immersing NCU-100a in water and five common organic solvents for one month, the excellent structure stability was verified by the intact PXRD patterns (Figure S24). The influence of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) separation performances has also been evaluated using a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (25/25/50, v/v/v) mixture at a flow rate of 2 \(\mathrm{mL\,min^{-1}}\) at \(298~\mathrm{K}.\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) almost concurrently eluted through the fixed bed very quickly, and then \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) breakthrough
after a significant retention time, indicating a minor influence on separation performance by gas impurities (Figures 5c and S25).
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, a flexible-robust analog of UTSA-300-Zn with extended pore spaces has been synthesized by using a different metal ion for removal of trace \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (1:99, v/v) mixtures. The robust pore space is indeed essential for trace gas impurities removal. The rational substitution of zinc nodes to copper ones expands the internal cavities to size-matching for acetylene accommodation, while the strong binding sites from hexafluorosilicate afford tight binding affinity and thus lead to an unprecedented sieving separation for trace acetylene removal from ethylene. It should be noted that the potential of flexible MOFs for gas separation has basically been underestimated, and this class of MOFs is less-explored. This work illustrates an excellent example of the rational design of flexible-robust MOFs for challenging gas separation or purification and thus will enlarge separation schemes for important applications.
